WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:09.759 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my name is Karen Lynn I'm the general  manager here at sukkah my second why is  a modern Japanese izakaya located on a  Lower East Side of Manhattan so the walk  yukata Sandow is a beef cutlet sandwich  katsu is cutlet in Japanese Sandow is  short for sandwich and Wagyu is a high  quality beef from Japanese cattle it is  really expensive because of the amount  of time and effort it goes into raising  the cattle and the result is beautiful  tender delicious flavorful meat the  marbling is definitely a lot more than  your ordinary beef there's a lot more  depth and flavor in the fat itself katsu  Sando is a comfort food in Japan you  know you can get at Japanese deli but  you can also get it at a high-end  restaurant so it could be very gourmet  or just everyday like street food  so we get a nice cut of strip point we  use a five Miyazaki Wagyu from Miyazaki  Prefecture it grades on yield and  quality so a is the highest yield grade  and five is the quality of the marbling  so Ava is the highest rating for Wagyu  after we cut it it's very simple we just  season it with salt and pepper we do the  batter with the egg and then we put the  breading on it the breading is panco  which is a japanese-style  breadcrumbs and then after that you know  it's ready to go in the fryer and that  part is actually the probably the most  difficult part making sure that it's  coming out at the right temperature in  the fryer that's where the skill comes  into and making a good cuts angle after  the cut so is fried we put the sauce on  it  tonkatsu sauce is very important any  kind of cuts in the dish there's grated  onion in it some ketchup mustard  mayonnaise a little Wurster sauce and I  think a lot of times if the sauce isn't  good it just doesn't compliment the meat  and we figured out a good blend of  tonkatsu sauce the bread that we use is  Japanese milk bread which is in Japan  called sugar palm it's a little bit  fluffier and sweeter and we definitely  want to toast it gives a little bit more  crunch to the bread then we spread a  mummy butter on it there's a little bit  of chicken fat in it so it kind of gives  that mummy punch then we assemble the  sandwich and cut off the crust and  present them on the plate while you just  started coming in I think five six years  ago and I think because of social media  and the internet you know like everyone  knows about it now and you know looking  into this beef katsu Sando and trying it  on our own it's good it's really  delicious this is a take on Japanese  comfort food that people haven't really  explored as much and we're excited that  we're one of the first in New York to  explore that and really share it with  in New York  [Music]

